{"id": "swebq0069", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "never", "audio_s": 2.32, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2625, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "In 1912, Lenin, who was forced to flee the country, chose him to serve on the first Central Committee of the Bolshevik party, which had now broken from the Social Democrats.", "query": "what led to stalin rise in power?", "transcript": "What led to Stalin's rise in power?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Stalin's rise to power was a long process that began after the October Revolution of 1917. At first he was a committed revolutionary but not yet a leader. He joined the Bolshevik Party and worked closely with Lenin. After Lenin died in 1924, Stalin gradually outmaneuvered his rivals. He controlled the Communist Party through propaganda and purges. By the late 1920s he had eliminated most of his opponents. In 1929 he launched the Five-Year Plans which focused on rapid industrialization and collectivization of agriculture. These policies led to widespread suffering but also increased central control. By the mid-1930s Stalin was firmly in power and ruled as dictator until his death in 1953.", "answer_ms": 5531}
